# 4. Source router

This is where we stop treating “web search” as the universal evidence source.

| Claim                               | First source                    |
| ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| country/capital/geographic relation | World Bank / Wikidata           |
| structured entity relation          | Wikidata                        |
| paper/author/publication            | OpenAlex                        |
| scholarly literature discovery      | OpenAlex → OpenAIRE             |
| DOI/retraction/correction           | Crossref                        |
| software release/version            | GitHub / package registry       |
| vulnerability                       | NVD                             |
| public-company filing               | SEC EDGAR                       |
| population/economic statistic       | World Bank                      |
| current general fact                | Tavily                          |
| contested/current general fact      | Tavily + Brave                  |
| interpretive scholarly claim        | OpenAlex/OpenAIRE → source text |
| page-specific claim                 | direct extract                  |
| JS-only inaccessible claim          | browser last                    |

That means your Abhinavagupta example:

```text
"Abhinavagupta's theory of rasa says
consciousness relishes in itself."
```

gets:

```text
domain = SCHOLARLY / INDIAN_PHILOSOPHY
type   = SCHOLARLY_ATTRIBUTION
freshness = STATIC
```

Then:

```text
OpenAlex semantic/text search
       +
OpenAIRE
       ↓
identify scholarship / edition / translation
       ↓
retrieve actual relevant passage
       ↓
FactJudge:
does this evidence actually justify
that paraphrase?
```

OpenAlex currently gives a free API key **$1/day of API usage**, enough for roughly 1,000 searches/day or 10,000 list/filter calls/day; singleton lookups are free. It also has semantic search. ([OpenAlex][6])

OpenAIRE's current V3 Graph API covers research products, projects, organizations, data sources and persons; its public API terms say authenticated access can go up to 7,200 requests/hour, with lower unauthenticated limits. ([OpenAIRE Graph][7])

Crossref is free with no signup and is particularly useful for DOI metadata, corrections/retractions and publisher metadata. ([www.crossref.org][8])

