# 5. Free/cheap starting executor pool

This is enough to build v0 without spending much.

| Source           | Use                           | Current free capacity                                            |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Tavily**       | generic web + extraction      | 1,000 credits/month, no card                                     |
| **Brave Search** | independent second web index  | $5 monthly free credit = ~1,000 Search requests at current price |
| **OpenAlex**     | academic                      | $1 free usage/day                                                |
| **OpenAIRE**     | academic graph                | free public API                                                  |
| **Crossref**     | scholarly metadata            | free/no signup                                                   |
| **Wikidata**     | structured entities/relations | public CC0 interfaces                                            |
| **World Bank**   | country/economic facts        | free/no authentication                                           |
| **GitHub REST**  | software/repos/releases       | 60/hour unauth; 5,000/hour authenticated                         |
| **NVD**          | CVEs/security                 | free; 5/30 sec public, 50/30 sec with free key                   |
| **SEC EDGAR**    | official company filings      | free; fair-use max 10 req/s                                      |

Tavily is particularly good for the initial hot path. Its `fast` and `ultra-fast` search depths cost one credit, while `advanced` costs two. It can also do query-focused extraction, map and crawl. ([Tavily Docs][9])

Brave currently gives $5/month in credits; its Search endpoint costs $5 per 1,000 calls and supports 50 QPS. That's excellent as an independent index for contradiction-sensitive checks. ([Brave][10])

GitHub's normal authenticated REST allowance is 5,000 calls/hour. ([GitHub Docs][11]) NVD gives 5 requests/30 seconds publicly or 50/30 seconds with a free API key. ([NVD][12])

Wikidata exposes public search, entity access, SPARQL and REST interfaces and its data is CC0. ([Wikidata][13])

SEC permits free programmatic EDGAR access with a current fair-access ceiling of 10 requests/second. ([SEC][14])

### One caution on Brave

Their standard Search terms distinguish plans with storage rights. If we cache evidence permanently, **don't assume Brave snippets themselves can be stored indefinitely**. Store the source URL and independently fetched source evidence unless your plan grants storage rights. ([Brave][10])

