# 19. Papers the coding agent should actually implement mechanisms from

### **FIRE — implement immediately**

Core idea:

```text
retrieve
→ verify
→ confident?
   yes stop
   no generate next retrieval
```

This is probably the most directly important paper for our optimizer. ([ACL Anthology][19])

[FIRE paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00784?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

---

### **LLMCompiler — steal the scheduler architecture**

Use:

```text
planner
→ dependency DAG
→ task fetching
→ parallel executor
```

It reported up to 3.7× latency speedup and 6.7× cost reduction over ReAct in its experiments. ([Proceedings of Machine Learning Research][20])

[LLMCompiler paper/code reference](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/kim24y.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

For FactMiner we already know most of the DAG, so ours can be even simpler.

---

### **Claimify — improve compilation**

Take:

```text
ambiguity detection
decontextualization
coverage
atomicity
```

rather than blindly splitting sentences. ([arXiv][4])

---

### **Optimizing Decomposition for Optimal Claim Verification**

Implement only after baseline.

Key insight:

> decomposition should be optimized for the downstream verifier, not for some abstract notion of maximum atomicity.

It reports average accuracy improvements of 0.12 across its settings. ([ACL Anthology][5])

---

### **ClaimCheck**

Especially relevant to our “cheap model inside good program” thesis.

Its pipeline separates targeted question generation, web evidence retrieval, answer extraction and verdict synthesis and demonstrated small-model viability. ([ACL Anthology][21])

The later ClaimCheck version reports Qwen3-4B achieving 76.4% on AVeriTeC using modular live-Web verification. ([arXiv][22])

---

### **FaStFact**

Steal two mechanisms:

```text
confidence-based pre-verification
document-level evidence rather than one-line SERP snippets
```

Its explicit target is reducing unnecessary web search/inference while retaining factual evaluation quality. ([arXiv][23])

---

### **GAVEL**

Implement:

```text
atomic subclaim
→ exact evidence binding
→ mechanized citation/span verification
```

This should become our evidence admission layer. ([ACL Anthology][16])

---

### **Ev2R**

Very important scientifically because **verdict accuracy alone isn't enough to evaluate retrieval**.

Ev2R evaluates evidence quality jointly with its ability to support the verdict and shows stronger agreement with human assessment than older retrieval metrics. ([ACL Anthology][24])

Use it as inspiration for:

```text
evidence_score
≠
verdict_score
```

---

### **HARIS**

Use later for hard multi-hop claims.

Its architecture explicitly separates high-level verification-chain reasoning from low-level iterative search. ([arXiv][25])

That maps to:

```text
FactMiner policy
   ↓
missing fact?
   ↓
SearchWorker
   ↓
evidence
   ↓
resume verification
```

---

### **RouteLLM**

Use for model nodes only.

RouteLLM learns when a weaker model is good enough versus when a stronger one is worth its cost. ([arXiv][26])

For us:

```text
claim compiler → cheap model usually
hard interpretation → stronger model
query rewrite → cheap
structured lookup → no LLM
```

Don't use RouteLLM as the whole policy.

---

# 20. The first complete system I would actually ship

Not the theoretically ultimate one.

**FactMiner v0.1:**

```text
FastAPI
+
SQLite evidence/event store
+
HydraDB projection
+
frozen FactJudge
+
Tavily fast
+
World Bank
+
Wikidata
+
OpenAlex
+
OpenAIRE
+
Crossref
+
GitHub
+
NVD
+
asyncio TaskGroup
```

No Playwright initially.

No RL.

No proactive crawling.

No large knowledge ingestion.

Then get **RET-000 working end-to-end**.

Once it produces a frozen baseline, Cogym begins removing work:

```text
Do we need four searches?
Do we need Extract?
Do we need FactJudge here?
Should this route to OpenAlex?
Can this stop already?
Can these requests run simultaneously?
Can the graph answer this?
Can the cheap model do this node?
```

And every time the answer is yes **without degrading factual quality**, the miner gets cheaper and faster.

That is the system's actual evolutionary mechanism:

> **start with an intentionally high-recall verifier, then use Cogym to compress away unnecessary cognition and retrieval while factual accuracy remains a hard invariant.**

That is a much stronger experimental thesis than merely trying to make FactJudge itself score another 0.5%.

